sumption_ids":[],"boundary":"The evidence mapping records a Lean statement that implies the printed one. Lean status per paper is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.thm-one-sided-finite-decision-boundary"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.thm-one-sided-finite-decision-boundary"],"id":"long.thm-one-sided-finite-decision-boundary","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 9.48 (C: the one-sided finite decision boundary)","locator":"Long record Theorem 9.48, p. 111, label thm:one-sided-finite-decision-boundary","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*The equivalence of Theorem 5.10 separates two logically different kinds of evidence. A fatal greedy gap found at a finite rank is a finite certificate that $1/2\\notin\\mathcal A$. By contrast, membership requires survival at every rank; this paper supplies no finite certificate that the orbit survives forever and no completion theorem turning a long surviving prefix into membership.*\n\n*The reset-crossing hypotheses, truncation-rung ladder, and sharp-capacity inequalities feed sufficient conditions for membership. They are not proved equivalent to one another, nor is failure to find one at a given depth evidence of survival. Consequently these searches give at most a semi-decision procedure for *nonmembership of the specific value $1/2$*. Halting does not prove the universal statement in \\#257, and non-halting does not prove that $1/2$ is represented. In particular, neither truth nor falsity of the universal problem is shown semi-decidable here. Deeper negative search alone establishes nothing beyond the tested finite ranks; this is the quantifier boundary of Theorem 2.11.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.thm-one-sided-finite-decision-boundary","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:264b08973a5f23b2f98ad989b0c1708096513d86929f19048623a2a9fe18092c/nodes/long.thm-one-sided-finite-d